# Frontend parity rule

The repository ships two frontends:

- `frontend/full-version/` — the reference implementation (Materio
  Next.js admin template, complete feature set).
- `frontend/starter-kit/` — the active development surface, a trimmed
  version of the same template.

`starter-kit` MUST stay structurally and stylistically identical to
`full-version` so that future template upgrades and cross-port merges
are mechanical.

## The single intentional difference

`full-version` ships with **mock / static / fake data** (fixtures,
fake API routes under `src/app/api/*`, hardcoded arrays in
`src/data/`, `src/fake-db/`, etc.).

`starter-kit` MUST instead source **all dynamic data from the Django
backend** (DRF, sessions, allauth headless API, REST endpoints under
`/api/...`). This is the only sanctioned divergence from
`full-version`.

When porting a component / page / view from `full-version` to
`starter-kit`:

- Keep file location, name, exports, props, types, and JSX/markup
  identical.
- Replace mock fetches and local fake arrays with real calls to the
  Django backend (server-side `fetch` in RSC, route handlers, or
  client-side via the shared API client).
- Do not copy `full-version`'s `src/fake-db/`, `src/app/api/`,
  or in-file mock arrays. Replace them with backend calls.

### Data contract direction: frontend-first

**The frontend is the source of truth for the API contract.** The
shape the component already consumes in `full-version` (its props,
types under `src/types/`, mock fixtures under `src/fake-db/` /
`src/data/`) IS the target shape of the Django REST endpoint.

- **Change Django to fit the frontend**, not the other way around.
  When wiring a `starter-kit` component to Django, reshape the DRF
  serializer / endpoint / response so it returns exactly what the
  `full-version` mock returned (same field names, casing, nesting,
  enums, pagination envelope, error format).
- Treat `full-version`'s mock data and TypeScript types as the
  **specification** for the matching DRF serializer. If a serializer
  exists that does not match — adjust the serializer (rename fields,
  add `SerializerMethodField`s, reshape nested objects) until it
  matches the mock 1:1.
- Use `to_representation` / serializer-level renaming / `source=`
  arguments on Django side to align field names with the frontend
  shape; do not rename in TypeScript.
- Frontend-side adapters are a **last resort**, allowed only when the
  desired shape is genuinely impossible on the backend (e.g. it would
  break another stable consumer, or violate a Django invariant).
  In that case document the reason in a code comment at the adapter.
- New endpoints MUST be designed by first looking at the corresponding
  `full-version` mock / TypeScript type and matching it exactly.
- Update OpenAPI schema accordingly so generated types in
  `src/lib/api/schema.ts` line up with the `full-version`-style types
  the components already use.

## Hard rules

1. **Look at `full-version` first.** Before adding or changing
   anything under `frontend/starter-kit/`, locate the corresponding
   path in `frontend/full-version/` and use it as the source of truth
   for structure, naming, props, and styling.
2. **Mirror the folder layout.** Keep the same directory tree:
   `src/@core/`, `src/@layouts/`, `src/@menu/`, `src/components/`,
   `src/views/`, `src/app/`, `src/hooks/`, `src/libs/`, `src/utils/`,
   `src/configs/`, `src/types/`, `src/data/`, etc. Do not invent new
   top-level folders that do not exist in `full-version`.
3. **Mirror file names and exports.** A component, hook, type,
   constant, or page that exists in both projects MUST have the same
   filename, default/named export, and public signature in
   `starter-kit` as in `full-version`.
4. **Mirror import paths and aliases.** Keep `tsconfig.json` path
   aliases (`@/`, `@core/`, `@layouts/`, `@menu/`, `@views/`,
   `@components/`, `@configs/`) and import styles aligned. Do not
   change an alias in `starter-kit` without changing it in
   `full-version` first.
5. **Reuse, do not reinvent.** If `full-version` already implements
   the component / hook / util you need, port it as-is (copy the file,
   keep the same internals) instead of writing a new variant. Only
   swap the data source from mock to Django (see "single intentional
   difference" above).
6. **Allowed divergence.** `starter-kit` is intentionally smaller —
   missing features, demos, or locales are fine. When you must
   diverge:
   - Keep names and paths identical to `full-version`.
   - Prefer omission over renaming.
   - Add a short comment `// starter-kit: trimmed from full-version`
     when a file is a reduced version of its `full-version` twin.
7. **New features.** If a feature does not yet exist in either
   project, design it in `starter-kit` using the same conventions
   (folder placement, component shape, naming, styling, import
   aliases) as comparable features in `full-version`.

## Sanctioned exceptions (new / extended logic)

The mirroring rules above apply to anything that already exists in
`full-version`. They MUST NOT block legitimately new business logic.
Divergence is **allowed and expected** when:

- A new feature is required by SEOPartnerHub that has no counterpart
  in `full-version` (e.g. SEO-specific dashboards, outreach pipelines,
  partner workflows, role-based admin screens).
- An existing `full-version` component is being **extended** with
  additional fields, actions, columns, filters, statuses, or related
  entities that the mock simply did not have.
- The feature requires **new Django endpoints** (URLs, viewsets,
  serializers, services, tasks) that do not exist in the
  `full-version` mocks at all.
- The feature requires **extra fields** on an existing endpoint /
  serializer / type to support the SEOPartnerHub use case.

In those cases:

- Still mirror `full-version` conventions for naming, folder
  placement, component composition, styling, import aliases, and
  TypeScript patterns.
- Place new files in the directory where `full-version` would have
  placed them by analogy (e.g. a new dashboard view goes under
  `src/views/<feature>/`, a new layout piece under `src/@layouts/`,
  a new typed DTO under `src/types/`).
- Extend types / DTOs additively. Prefer adding optional fields on
  existing shapes over forking parallel types.
- Reuse `full-version` primitive components (cards, tables, dialogs,
  form controls) as building blocks; do not introduce a parallel UI
  kit.
- New Django endpoints MUST still follow the **frontend-first
  contract direction** above: design the response shape from the
  frontend's perspective (props, generated OpenAPI types) and then
  shape the DRF serializer to match it.
- Document the divergence briefly in code (`// new feature: <reason>`
  or `// extends full-version <component>`) so future merges with
  template updates are unambiguous.

## Workflow checklist

Before finishing a change under `frontend/starter-kit/`:

- [ ] Checked the same path in `frontend/full-version/`.
- [ ] File name, exports, props, and types match `full-version`
      (or there is a documented reason they cannot).
- [ ] Folder placement matches `full-version`.
- [ ] Import aliases and paths match `full-version`.
- [ ] No new top-level folders were introduced.
- [ ] No renames of components, hooks, or utilities that exist in
      `full-version`.
- [ ] No mock data, `fake-db`, or fake API routes were copied over;
      all dynamic data is fetched from the Django backend.
- [ ] Django endpoint / serializer was reshaped (if needed) to return
      the exact field names and structure that `full-version` mocks
      used; no frontend-side adapter was added unless impossible on
      the backend (with reason documented at the call site).
- [ ] If the change is new / extended logic with no `full-version`
      twin, the "Sanctioned exceptions" section above was followed:
      naming, placement, aliases, and UI primitives mirror
      `full-version`; new Django endpoints were still designed
      frontend-first; divergence is documented in code.

## Forbidden

- Creating files in `frontend/starter-kit/` with names, locations, or
  exports that differ from an existing twin in `frontend/full-version/`.
- Introducing new path aliases, folder roots, or naming conventions in
  `starter-kit` that are absent from `full-version`.
- Rewriting a component from scratch when an equivalent exists in
  `full-version` — port it instead.
- Copying `src/fake-db/`, `src/data/*` fixtures used as runtime data,
  or fake API route handlers from `full-version`. Use the Django
  backend instead.
- Renaming a field, changing casing, or restructuring a payload in
  TypeScript to "make Django data fit" a `full-version` component.
  Change the DRF serializer instead so the wire format matches the
  `full-version` shape directly.
